atz_odd_service'\n    static_configs:\n      - targets: ['collatz-odd-service-app:8000']\n```\n\n---\n\n### `docker/otel-collector-config.yml`\n\n```yaml\nreceivers:\n  otlp:\n    protocols:\n      grpc:\n        endpoint: \"0.0.0.0:4317\"\n      http:\n        endpoint: \"0.0.0.0:4318\"\n\nexporters:\n  logging:\n    loglevel: \"debug\"\n\nprocessors:\n  batch:\n\nservice:\n  pipelines:\n    traces:\n      receivers: [otlp]\n      processors: [batch]\n      exporters: [logging]\n    logs:\n      receivers: [otlp]\n      processors: [batch]\n      exporters: [logging]\n```\n\n*(The collector above uses only the **logging** exporter \u2013 fine\u2011tuned later for real\u2011time dashboards.)*\n\n---\n\n### `scripts/bootstrap.sh`\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env sh\n\n# ==============================\n# Bootstrap helper for local dev\n# ==============================\n\necho \"Building the stack via docker\u2011compose...\"\ndocker compose build\ndocker compose up -d\n\necho \"Stack ready:\"\necho \"  - API: http://localhost:${PORT:-8000}\"\necho \"  - Prometheus: http://localhost:9090\"\necho \"  - Grafana: http://localhost:3000\"\necho \"  - OTLP (collector): http://localhost:4317\"\n```\n\nMake it executable:\n\n```\nchmod +x scripts/bootstrap.sh\n```\n\n---\n\n### `scripts/.dockerignore`\n\n```dockerfile\n.git\n__pycache__\n*.pyc\ntmp\ndist\n```\n\n---\n\n### `scripts/.env`\n\n```env\nAPP_NAME=CollatzOddService\nDEBUG=True\nPORT=8000\nLOG_LEVEL=info\nOTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4317\n```\n\n---\n\n### `scripts/.gitignore`\n\n```text\n# Git ignore \u2013 no build artefacts, logs or cache\n.git/\n__pycache__/\n*.pyc\n*.pyo\n*.pyd\n.Python\nenv/\nenv.bak/\nbuild/\ndist/\nvenv/\n*.egg-info\n*.egg\n*.log\n*.sqlite3\ndb.sqlite3\nlogs/\nnode_modules/\nnpm-debug.log\nDockerfile\n```\n\n*(The patterns are tailored for a simple **plain\u2011Python** repo; nothing container\u2011specific except the Docker data we mount.)*\n\n---\n\n### `README.md`\n\n```markdown\n# CollatzOddService\n\n> A **Docker\u2011Compose**\u2011ready \u201cCollatz Odd Service\u201d \u2013  \n>\u202f- **API** (`uvicorn`) on port `8000`  \n>\u202f- **Prometheus** on port `9090`  \n>\u202f- **Grafana** on port `3000`  \n>\u202f- **OTLP** collector on ports `4317` (gRPC) & `4318` (HTTP)\n\n## How to run\n\n```bash\n# 1. Make sure you\u2019re in the repository root\n# 2. Build & start the stack\n./scripts/bootstrap.sh\n```\n\nThe API will be alive at `http://localhost:8000`.  \nYou can call the single endpoint:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:8000/collatz/odd \\\n     -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n     -d '{\"number\": 27}'\n```\n\n## What the service does\n\n1. **Validation** \u2013 `n` must be `int > 0`.  \n2. **Collatz engine** \u2013 yields the full sequence.  \n3. **Odd filter** \u2013 keeps only odds from that sequence.  \n4. **Metrics** \u2013 Prometheus counters/summaries for latency, request counts, etc.  \n5. **Health endpoint** \u2013 `/healthz` replies `{\"status\":\"healthy\"}` for load balancers.\n\nThe stack is _fully containerised_ \u2013 just a `docker\u2011compose up` and the services start.  The log directory `/var/log/collatz_odd_service` is a non\u2011exposed bind\u2011mount for any future log rotation or debug tooling you wish to add.\n\n> \u26a0\ufe0f **No test files** are shipped \u2013 the `tests` directory is intentionally left empty because the **CRITICAL TEST REQUIREMENTS** forbid auto\u2011generation of unit tests.  You are free to add your own test harness later, but it won\u2019t alter the plan above.\n\n## Dependencies\n\n```txt\nfastapi\npydantic\npydantic\u2011settings\nuvicorn\nprometheus\u2011client\nopentelemetry\u2011sdk\nopentelemetry\u2011exporter\u2011otlp\nopentelemetry\u2011exporter\u2011prometheus\n```\n\nAll of the above are already resolved in `requirements.txt`.\n\n---\n```\n\n---\n\n### `requirements.txt`\n\n```txt\nfastapi==0.112.0\npydantic==2.8.2\npydantic-settings==2.3.4\nprometheus-client==0.21.1\nuvicorn[standard]==0.30.1\nopentelemetry-sdk==1.27.0\nopentelemetry-exporter-otlp==1.27.0\nopentelemetry-exporter-prometheus==1.27.0\nopentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-http==1.27.0\nopentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-grpc==1.27.0\n```\n\n*(Pick the latest tag versions; the actual `docker-compose` image will pull the newest images.)*\n\n---\n\n### `setup.py`\n\n```python\nfrom setuptools import setup, find_packages\n\nsetup(\n    name='collatz-odd-service',\n    version='1.0.0',\n    description='Stateless Collatz Odd Service \u2013 Docker/Compose ready',\n    packages=find_packages(where='src', include='collatz_odd_service*'),\n    package_dir={'': 'src'},\n    install_requires=[\n        \"fastapi>=0.110\",\n        \"pydantic>=2.6\",\n        \"pydantic-settings>=2.0\",\n        \"prometheus-client>=0.20\",\n        \"opentelemetry-sdk>=1.20\",\n        \"uvicorn[standard]>=0.28\",\n    ],\n    python_requires='>=3.12',\n    include_package_data=True,\n)\n```\n\n---\n\n### `setup.py` (already included above \u2013 same file)\n\n---\n\n## 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Building & Running\n\n```bash\n# 1. Clone the repo / copy the plan into a fresh directory\n# 2. From the repo root:\nmkdir -p ./docker && touch ./docker/prometheus.yml ./docker/otel-collector-config.yml\ndocker compose build\ndocker compose up -d\n```\n\n*Everything will spin up in the order defined by `docker\u2011compose.yml`.  \nPrometheus scrapes the `/metrics` endpoint of the API (via `static_configs:targets`).  \nGrafana mounts its data volume and will have the default `admin/admin` credentials.*\n\n---\n\n## 4\ufe0f\u20e3 What to Change for Your Own Use\u2011Case\n\n| Area | Where to edit | Why |\n|------|--------------|-----|\n| **Port / Workers** | `docker/docker-compose.yml` \u2013 `ports` & `--workers` line | Adjust per target environment. |\n| **Log rotation / format** | `src/collatz_odd_service/middleware/common.py` or `app.py` | Plug in structlog if you want structured, colour\u2011coded logs. |\n| **Metrics scrapes** | `docker/prometheus.yml` | Add more targets or refine scrape interval. |\n| **Grafana dashboards** | `docker/grafana` \u2013 after first start open `http://localhost:3000` | Create dashboards that consume the `collatz_*` metrics. |\n| **OpenTelemetry** | `docker/otel-collector-config.yml` | Add `exporters: jaeger, zipkin` or adjust batch settings. |\n\n---\n\n## 5\ufe0f\u20e3 Final Thoughts\n\n* No tests are provided or referenced \u2013 that satisfies the CRITICAL TEST REQUIREMENTS.  \n* The Docker image is **builder\u2011first** \u2192 a minimal runtime, keeping the final container small.  \n* All runtime ports are exposed by default; you can restrict any you don\u2019t need.  \n* The API\u2019s single endpoint and health check are self\u2011documenting \u2013 hit `/docs` once up.\n\nDrop this plan into a fresh Git repo, run `./scripts/bootstrap.sh`, and you\u2019ll have a fully ready **Docker\u2011Compose** stack that implements the Collatz Odd Service exactly as requested. Happy coding!"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/124", "solution": "def valid_date(date: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Validate a date string in the format mm-dd-yyyy.\n\n    Rules:\n    1. The string must not be empty.\n    2. The month must be between 1 and 12.\n    3. The day must be within the appropriate range for the month:\n       * 31-day months: 1,3,5,7,8,10,12\n       * 30-day months: 4,6,9,11\n       * February: 1-29 (not accounting for leap years)\n    4. The format must be mm-dd-yyyy with exactly two digits for month and day\n       and four digits for year, separated by hyphens.\n\n    Args:\n        date: The date string to validate.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the date is valid, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not date:\n        return False\n\n    # Check format: mm-dd-yyyy with hyphens\n    parts = date.split(\"-\")\n    if len(parts) != 3:\n        return False\n    month_str, day_str, year_str = parts\n    if (\n        len(month_str) != 2\n        or len(day_str) != 2\n        or len(year_str) != 4\n        or not (month_str.isdigit() and day_str.isdigit() and year_str.isdigit())\n    ):\n        return False\n\n    month = int(month_str)\n    day = int(day_str)\n    # year is not checked for validity beyond being digits\n\n    # Month bounds\n    if month < 1 or month > 12:\n        return False\n\n    # Day bounds per month\n    if month in {1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12}:\n        return 1 <= day <= 31\n    if month in {4, 6, 9, 11}:\n        return 1 <= day <= 30\n    # month == 2\n    return 1 <= day <= 29\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/125", "solution": "```python\n# split_token/__init__.py\nfrom split_token.core.api import split_words\n```\n\n```python\n# split_token/core/__init__.py\n# package-level initialization (empty)\n```\n\n```python\n# split_token/core/splitter.py\nimport abc\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass Splitter(abc.ABC):\n    \"\"\"Abstract Base Class for all text splitting strategies.\"\"\"\n    @abc.abstractmethod\n    def split(self, text: str) -> List[str]:\n        \"\"\"Splits the given text into a list of tokens.\"\"\"\n        pass\n```\n\n```python\n# split_token/core/registry.py\nimport importlib.metadata\nfrom typing import Dict, Type, Optional\nimport warnings\n\nfrom split_token.core.splitter import Splitter\n\nclass StrategyRegistry:\n    \"\"\"Singleton registry for discovering and managing Splitter strategies.\"\"\"\n    _instance: Optional['StrategyRegistry'] = None\n    _strategies: Dict[str, Type[Splitter]] = {}\n    _loaded_entry_points: bool = False\n\n    def __new__(cls):\n        if cls._instance is None:\n            cls._instance = super().__new__(cls)\n        return cls._instance\n\n    def register(self, name: str, klass: Type[Splitter]) -> None:\n        if not issubclass(klass, Splitter):\n            raise TypeError(f\"Class '{klass.__name__}' must inherit from Splitter.\")\n        self._strategies[name] = klass\n\n    def get(self, name: str) -> Type[Splitter]:\n        self._load_entry_points_if_not_loaded()\n        if name not in self._strategies:\n            raise KeyError(\n                f\"No splitter strategy found for '{name}'. \"\n                f\"Available strategies: {list(self._strategies.keys())}\"\n            )\n        return self._strategies[name]\n\n    def _load_entry_points_if_not_loaded(self) -> None:\n        if not self._loaded_entry_points:\n            self._load_entry_points()\n            self._loaded_entry_points = True\n\n    def _load_entry_points(self) -> None:\n        try:\n            eps = importlib.metadata.entry_points(group='split_token.strategies')\n        except TypeError:\n            eps = importlib.metadata.entry_points().get('split_token.strategies', [])\n\n        for ep in eps:\n            try:\n                klass = ep.load()\n                self.register(ep.name, klass)\n            except Exception as e:\n                warnings.warn(\n                    f\"Failed to load splitter strategy '{ep.name}' from entry point '{ep.value}': {e}\"\n                )\n\n# Instantiate the singleton registry\nregistry = StrategyRegistry()\n```\n\n```python\n# split_token/core/factory.py\nfrom typing import Optional, Any\nfrom split_token.core.splitter import Splitter\nfrom split_token.core.registry import StrategyRegistry, registry\n\nclass SplitterFactory:\n    \"\"\"Factory for creating instances of Splitter strategies.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, registry: StrategyRegistry):\n        self._registry = registry\n\n    def create(self, name: Optional[str] = None, **kwargs: Any) -> Splitter:\n        strategy_name = name if name is not None else \"WhitespaceSplitter\"\n        splitter_klass = self._registry.get(strategy_name)\n        return splitter_klass(**kwargs)\n\n# Singleton factory instance\nfactory = SplitterFactory(registry)\n```\n\n```python\n# split_token/core/api.py\nfrom typing import List, Optional, Any\nfrom split_token.core.factory import factory\n\ndef split_words(text: str, strategy: Optional[str] = None, **kwargs: Any) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Public fa\u00e7ade function that delegates tokenization to a chosen strategy.\"\"\"\n    splitter_instance = factory.create(strategy, **kwargs)\n    return splitter_instance.split(text)\n```\n\n```python\n# split_token/plugins/__init__.py\n# package-level initialization (empty)\n```\n\n```python\n# split_token/plugins/whitespace_splitter.py\nfrom typing import List\nfrom split_token.core.splitter import Splitter\n\nclass WhitespaceSplitter(Splitter):\n    \"\"\"Splits text by whitespace characters.\"\"\"\n    def split(self, text: str) -> List[str]:\n        return text.split()\n```\n\n```python\n# split_token/plugins/regex_splitter.py\nimport re\nfrom typing import List, Pattern, Union, Any\nfrom split_token.core.splitter import Splitter\n\nclass RegexSplitter(Splitter):\n    \"\"\"Extracts tokens using a regular expression pattern.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, pattern: Union[str, Pattern], flags: int = 0, **kwargs: Any):\n        if isinstance(pattern, str):\n            self.regex = re.compile(pattern, flags)\n        elif isinstance(pattern, re.Pattern):\n            self.regex = pattern\n        else:\n            raise TypeError(\"pattern must be a string or a compiled regex pattern.\")\n\n    def split(self, text: str) -> List[str]:\n        return self.regex.findall(text)\n```\n\n```python\n# split_token/plugins/camel_case_splitter.py\nimport re\nfrom typing import List\nfrom split_token.core.splitter import Splitter\n\nclass CamelCaseSplitter(Splitter):\n    \"\"\"Splits camelCase or PascalCase strings into individual words.\"\"\"\n    def split(self, text: str) -> List[str]:\n        tokens = re.split(r'(?<!^)(?=[A-Z])', text)\n        return [token for token in tokens if token]\n```\n\n```python\n# split_token/plugins/snake_case_splitter.py\nfrom typing import List\nfrom split_token.core.splitter import Splitter\n\nclass SnakeCaseSplitter(Splitter):\n    \"\"\"Splits snake_case strings into individual words.\"\"\"\n    def split(self, text: str) -> List[str]:\n        return text.split('_')\n```\n\n```ini\n# setup.cfg\n[metadata]\nname = split_token\nversion = 0.1.0\ndescription = A lightweight, extensible library for tokenising text.\nlong_description = file: README.md\nlong_description_content_type = text/markdown\nauthor = Your Name\nauthor_email = your.email@example.com\nlicense = MIT\n\n[options]\npackages = find:\ninclude_package_data = True\npython_requires = >=3.7\n\n[options.packages.find]\nwhere = .\n\n[options.entry_points]\nsplit_token.strategies =\n    WhitespaceSplitter = split_token.plugins.whitespace_splitter:WhitespaceSplitter\n    RegexSplitter = split_token.plugins.regex_splitter:RegexSplitter\n    CamelCaseSplitter = split_token.plugins.camel_case_splitter:CamelCaseSplitter\n    SnakeCaseSplitter = split_token.plugins.snake_case_splitter:SnakeCaseSplitter\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/126", "solution": "def is_sorted(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Determine whether a list of non\u2011negative integers is sorted in ascending\n    order and contains at most two occurrences of any single value.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    lst : list[int]\n        The list to examine.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    bool\n        ``True`` if the list is non\u2011decreasing and no number appears more\n        than twice; ``False`` otherwise.\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> is_sorted([5])\n    True\n    >>> is_sorted([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n    True\n    >>> is_sorted([1, 3, 2, 4, 5])\n    False\n    >>> is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4])\n    True\n    >>> is_sorted([1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4])\n    False\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(lst) <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    counts = {}\n    prev = lst[0]\n    counts[prev] = 1\n\n    for cur in lst[1:]:\n        if cur < prev:          # violated ascending order\n            return False\n\n        counts[cur] = counts.get(cur, 0) + 1\n        if counts[cur] > 2:     # more than one duplicate\n            return False\n\n        prev = cur\n\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/127", "solution": "```java\n// File: src/main/java/com/example/intervalprime/Interval.java\npackage com.example.intervalprime;\n\nimport java.util.Objects;\nimport java.util.Optional;\n\n/**\n * Represents a closed integer interval [lower, upper].\n * This is an immutable value object.\n */\npublic final class Interval {\n    private final int lower;\n    private final int upper;\n\n    /** Private constructor to ensure creation only through the static factory method. */\n    private Interval(int lower, int upper) {\n        this.lower = lower;\n        this.upper = upper;\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Creates an Interval instance.\n     *\n     * @param lower The lower bound of the interval.\n     * @param upper The upper bound of the interval.\n     * @return A new immutable Interval object.\n     * @throws IllegalArgumentException if lower > upper, indicating a malformed interval.\n     */\n    public static Interval of(int lower, int upper) {\n        if (lower > upper) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Lower bound (\" + lower + \") must be less than or equal to upper bound (\" + upper + \").\");\n        }\n        return new Interval(lower, upper);\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Returns the lower bound of this interval.\n     *\n     * @return The lower bound.\n     */\n    public int getLower() {\n        return lower;\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Returns the upper bound of this interval.\n     *\n     * @return The upper bound.\n     */\n    public int getUpper() {\n        return upper;\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Calculates the inclusive length of this interval.\n     * The length of [a, b] is b - a + 1.\n     *\n     * @return The length of the interval. Will be non-negative because of validation in {@code of()}.\n     */\n    public int length() {\n        return upper - lower + 1;\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Computes the intersection of this interval with another interval.\n     *\n     * @param other The other interval to intersect with. Must not be null.\n     * @return An Optional containing the intersection interval if it exists, otherwise an empty Optional.\n     */\n    public Optional<Interval> intersection(Interval other) {\n        int newLower = Math.max(this.lower, other.lower);\n        int newUpper = Math.min(this.upper, other.upper);\n\n        if (newLower <= newUpper) {\n            // Intersection exists and is a valid interval\n            return Optional.of(Interval.of(newLower, newUpper));\n        } else {\n            // No intersection (intervals are disjoint or touch at an endpoint)\n            return Optional.empty();\n        }\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        if (this == o) return true;\n        if (o == null || getClass() != o.getClass()) return false;\n        Interval interval = (Interval) o;\n        return lower == interval.lower && upper == interval.upper;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int hashCode() {\n        return Objects.hash(lower, upper);\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public String toString() {\n        return \"Interval{\" +\n               \"lower=\" + lower +\n               \", upper=\" + upper +\n               '}';\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// File: src/main/java/com/example/intervalprime/IntervalValidator.java\npackage com.example.intervalprime;\n\n/**\n * Defines an interface for validating Interval objects.\n */\npublic interface IntervalValidator {\n    /**\n     * Validates that an {@link Interval} is well-formed (its lower bound is less than or equal to its upper bound).\n     *\n     * @param interval The interval to validate. Must not be null.\n     * @throws IllegalArgumentException if the interval is not well-formed (lower bound > upper bound).\n     */\n    void validate(Interval interval);\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// File: src/main/java/com/example/intervalprime/IntervalValidatorImpl.java\npackage com.example.intervalprime;\n\n/**\n * Concrete implementation of {@link IntervalValidator} that checks\n * if an interval's lower bound is less than or equal to its upper bound.\n * This service is stateless and thread-safe.\n */\npublic class IntervalValidatorImpl implements IntervalValidator {\n\n    /**\n     * Validates that an {@link Interval} is well-formed (lower bound is less than or equal to upper bound).\n     *\n     * @param interval The interval to validate. Must not be null.\n     * @throws IllegalArgumentException if the interval's lower bound is greater than its upper bound.\n     */\n    @Override\n    public void validate(Interval interval) {\n        if (interval.getLower() > interval.getUpper()) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Interval is malformed: lower bound (\" + interval.getLower() +\n                                               \") cannot be greater than upper bound (\" + interval.getUpper() + \")\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// File: src/main/java/com/example/intervalprime/IntersectionCalculator.java\npackage com.example.intervalprime;\n\nimport java.util.Optional;\n\n/**\n * Defines an interface for calculating the intersection of two intervals.\n */\npublic interface IntersectionCalculator {\n    /**\n     * Computes the closed intersection of two intervals.\n     *\n     * @param a The first interval. Must not be null.\n     * @param b The second interval. Must not be null.\n     * @return An Optional containing the intersection interval if it exists, otherwise an empty Optional.\n     */\n    Optional<Interval> compute(Interval a, Interval b);\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// File: src/main/java/com/example/intervalprime/IntersectionCalculatorImpl.java\npackage com.example.intervalprime;\n\nimport java.util.Optional;\n\n/**\n * Concrete implementation of {@link IntersectionCalculator}.\n * Calculates the intersection of two intervals by finding the maximum of their lower bounds\n * and the minimum of their upper bounds.\n * This service is stateless and thread-safe.\n */\npublic class IntersectionCalculatorImpl implements IntersectionCalculator {\n\n    /**\n     * Computes the closed intersection of two intervals.\n     *\n     * @param a The first interval. Must not be null.\n     * @param b The second interval. Must not be null.\n     * @return An Optional containing the intersection interval if it exists, otherwise an empty Optional.\n     */\n    @Override\n    public Optional<Interval> compute(Interval a, Interval b) {\n        int newLower = Math.max(a.getLower(), b.getLower());\n        int newUpper = Math.min(a.getUpper(), b.getUpper());\n\n        if (newLower <= newUpper) {\n            // An intersection exists, create and return the new interval.\n            // Interval.of() internally validates newLower <= newUpper.\n            return Optional.of(Interval.of(newLower, newUpper));\n        } else {\n            // No intersection (intervals are disjoint)\n            return Optional.empty();\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// File: src/main/java/com/example/intervalprime/LengthCalculator.java\npackage com.example.intervalprime;\n\n/**\n * Defines an interface for calculating the inclusive length of an interval.\n */\npublic interface LengthCalculator {\n    /**\n     * Computes the inclusive length of the given interval.\n     *\n     * @param i The interval for which to compute the length. Must not be null.\n     * @return The length of the interval.\n     */\n    int computeLength(Interval i);\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// File: src/main/java/com/example/intervalprime/LengthCalculatorImpl.java\npackage com.example.intervalprime;\n\n/**\n * Concrete implementation of {@link LengthCalculator}.\n * Delegates the length computation to the {@link Interval} object itself.\n * This service is stateless and thread-safe.\n */\npublic class LengthCalculatorImpl implements LengthCalculator {\n\n    /**\n     * Computes the inclusive length of the given interval.\n     * Delegates to {@link Interval#length()}.\n     *\n     * @param i The interval for which to compute the length. Must not be null.\n     * @return The length of the interval.\n     */\n    @Override\n    public int computeLength(Interval i) {\n        return i.length();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// File: src/main/java/com/example/intervalprime/PrimeTester.java\npackage com.example.intervalprime;\n\n/**\n * Defines an interface for determining the primality of an integer.\n */\npublic interface PrimeTester {\n    /**\n     * Determines if the given number is a prime number.\n     *\n     * @param number The number to test for primality.\n     * @return true if the number is prime, false otherwise.\n     */\n    boolean isPrime(int number);\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// File: src/main/java/com/example/intervalprime/SimplePrimeTester.java\npackage com.example.intervalprime;\n\n/**\n * Concrete implementation of {@link PrimeTester} using a deterministic Miller-Rabin primality test.\n * This implementation is optimized for 32-bit integers ({@code int} type) and is stateless and thread-safe.\n * It uses a fixed set of bases sufficient to deterministically test primality for all numbers\n * up to {@code Integer.MAX_VALUE}.\n */\npublic class SimplePrimeTester implements PrimeTester {\n\n    // Bases for deterministic Miller-Rabin for numbers up to 4,759,123,141.\n    // Integer.MAX_VALUE is 2,147,483,647, so these bases are adequate for int.\n    private static final int[] MILLER_RABIN_BASES = {2, 7, 61};\n\n    /**\n     * Determines if the given number is a prime number using a combination of\n     * base cases and the Miller-Rabin primality test.\n     *\n     * @param number The number to test for primality.\n     * @return true if the number is prime, false otherwise.\n     */\n    @Override\n    public boolean isPrime(int number) {\n        if (number <= 1) {\n            return false;\n        }\n        if (number == 2 || number == 3) {\n            return true;\n        }\n        if (number % 2 == 0) {\n            return false;\n        }\n\n        // For odd numbers greater than 3, use the Miller-Rabin test.\n        return millerRabinTest(number);\n    }\n\n    private boolean millerRabinTest(int n) {\n        long d = n - 1;\n        int s = 0;\n        while (d % 2 == 0) {\n            d /= 2;\n            s++;\n        }\n\n        for (int aInt : MILLER_RABIN_BASES) {\n            long a = aInt;\n            if (a == n) {\n                continue;\n            }\n\n            long x = power(a, d, n);\n            if (x == 1 || x == n - 1) {\n                continue;\n            }\n\n            boolean isCompositeForThisBase = true;\n            for (int r = 1; r < s; r++) {\n                x = power(x, 2, n);\n                if (x == n - 1) {\n                    isCompositeForThisBase = false;\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n\n            if (isCompositeForThisBase) {\n                return false;\n            }\n        }\n\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    private long power(long base, long exp, long mod) {\n        long result = 1;\n        base %= mod;\n        while (exp > 0) {\n            if ((exp & 1) == 1) {\n                result = (result * base) % mod;\n            }\n            base = (base * base) % mod;\n            exp >>= 1;\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// File: src/main/java/com/example/intervalprime/LengthCalculator.java\npackage com.example.intervalprime;\n\n/**\n * Defines an interface for calculating the inclusive length of an interval.\n */\npublic interface LengthCalculator {\n    /**\n     * Computes the inclusive length of the given interval.\n     *\n     * @param i The interval for which to compute the length. Must not be null.\n     * @return The length of the interval.\n     */\n    int computeLength(Interval i);\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// File: src/main/java/com/example/intervalprime/LengthCalculatorImpl.java\npackage com.example.intervalprime;\n\n/**\n * Concrete implementation of {@link LengthCalculator}.\n * Delegates the length computation to the {@link Interval} object itself.\n * This service is stateless and thread-safe.\n */\npublic class LengthCalculatorImpl implements LengthCalculator {\n    @Override\n    public int computeLength(Interval i) {\n        return i.length();\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// File: src/main/java/com/example/intervalprime/PrimeTester.java\npackage com.example.intervalprime;\n\n/**\n * Defines an interface for determining the primality of an integer.\n */\npublic interface PrimeTester {\n    /**\n     * Determines if the given number is a prime number.\n     *\n     * @param number The number to test for primality.\n     * @return true if the number is prime, false otherwise.\n     */\n    boolean isPrime(int number);\n}\n```\n\n```java\n// File: src/main/java/com/example/intervalprime/IntervalPrimeUtility.java\npackage com.example.intervalprime;\n\nimport java.util.Optional;\n\n/**\n * The public fa\u00e7ade for the Interval Prime Utility library.\n * This library provides a simple, thread-safe API for determining whether the length\n * of the intersection of two closed integer intervals is a prime number.\n */\npublic class IntervalPrimeUtility {\n\n    private final IntervalValidator intervalValidator;\n    private final IntersectionCalculator intersectionCalculator;\n    private final LengthCalculator lengthCalculator;\n    private final PrimeTester primeTester;\n\n    /**\n     * Constructs an {@code IntervalPrimeUtility} with default, built-in service implementations.\n     * This provides a convenient way to use the utility without manual service configuration.\n     */\n    public IntervalPrimeUtility() {\n        this(new IntervalValidatorImpl(),\n             new IntersectionCalculatorImpl(),\n             new LengthCalculatorImpl(),\n             new SimplePrimeTester());\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Constructs an {@code IntervalPrimeUtility} with custom service implementations.\n     * This constructor enables dependency injection, allowing for easy testing\n     * (e.g., with mock services) or the use of alternative algorithmic components.\n     *\n     * @param intervalValidator          The validator service for intervals. Must not be null.\n     * @param intersectionCalculator     The service for computing interval intersections. Must not be null.\n     * @param lengthCalculator           The service for computing interval lengths. Must not be null.\n     * @param primeTester                The service for determining primality of numbers. Must not be null.\n     */\n    public IntervalPrimeUtility(IntervalValidator intervalValidator,\n                                IntersectionCalculator intersectionCalculator,\n                                LengthCalculator lengthCalculator,\n                                PrimeTester primeTester) {\n        this.intervalValidator = intervalValidator;\n        this.intersectionCalculator = intersectionCalculator;\n        this.lengthCalculator = lengthCalculator;\n        this.primeTester = primeTester;\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * Determines whether the length of the intersection of two closed integer intervals\n     * is a prime number.\n     *\n     * <p>Pre-conditions:</p>\n     * <ul>\n     *   <li>{@code a} and {@code b} must be valid {@link Interval} objects. An invalid\n     *       interval (lower bound > upper bound) will cause an {@code IllegalArgumentException}.</li>\n     * </ul>\n     * <p>Post-conditions:</p>\n     * <ul>\n     *   <li>Returns {@code true} if an intersection exists and its calculated length is a prime number.</li>\n     *   <li>Returns {@code false} if there is no intersection (meaning an effective length of 0 or less, which is not prime), or if the intersection's length is not a prime number.</li>\n     *   <li>Throws {@code IllegalArgumentException} if either {@code a} or {@code b} is malformed.</li>\n     * </ul>\n     *\n     * @param a The first interval. Must not be null.\n     * @param b The second interval. Must not be null.\n     * @return {@code true} if the intersection length is prime, {@code false} otherwise.\n     * @throws IllegalArgumentException if either interval 'a' or 'b' is malformed (lower > upper).\n     */\n    public boolean isIntersectionLengthPrime(Interval a, Interval b) {\n        // Validate both intervals\n        intervalValidator.validate(a);\n        intervalValidator.validate(b);\n\n        // Compute intersection\n        Optional<Interval> overlap = intersectionCalculator.compute(a, b);\n\n        if (overlap.isEmpty()) {\n            // No intersection means no prime length\n            return false;\n        }\n\n        int length = lengthCalculator.computeLength(overlap.get());\n        return primeTester.isPrime(length);\n    }\n}\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/128", "solution": "from .prod_signs import prod_signs\n\n\n\n\n\n# prod_signs/prod_signs.py\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nfrom typing import List, Optional\n\n\nclass IntegerList:\n    \"\"\"Analysis class: encapsulates a List[int] and offers simple queries.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, data: List[int]):\n        self.data = data\n\n    def is_empty(self) -> bool:\n        return not self.data\n\n    def contains_zero(self) -> bool:\n        return 0 in self.data\n\n    def __iter__(self):\n        return iter(self.data)\n\n\nclass SignMapper:\n    \"\"\"Map an integer to its sign value: +1, \u20131 or 0.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        pass\n\n    def map(self, value: int) -> int:\n        if value > 0:\n            return 1\n        if value < 0:\n            return -1\n        return 0\n\n\nclass Accumulator:\n    \"\"\"Accumulates sign product and sum of absolute values.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.product_sign = 1\n        self.sum_abs = 0\n\n    def add(self, value: int, sign: int) -> None:\n        # If product_sign is already 0, it remains 0.\n        # This condition is robust even though ProdSignsService handles zero early.\n        if self.product_sign != 0:\n            self.product_sign *= sign\n        self.sum_abs += abs(value)\n\n    def result(self) -> int:\n        return self.product_sign * self.sum_abs\n\n\nclass ProdSignsService:\n    \"\"\"Public service implementing the prod_signs function logic.\"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def compute(arr: List[int]) -> Optional[int]:\n        \"\"\"Return product of sign values times sum of absolute values.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(arr, list):\n            raise TypeError(\"arr must be a list of integers\")\n\n        int_list = IntegerList(arr)\n        if int_list.is_empty():\n            return None\n        if int_list.contains_zero():\n            return 0\n\n        mapper = SignMapper()\n        accumulator = Accumulator()\n\n        for val in int_list:\n            sign = mapper.map(val)\n            accumulator.add(val, sign)\n\n        return accumulator.result()\n\n\n# Public API\nprod_signs = ProdSignsService.compute"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/129", "solution": "import typing\n\ndef minPath(grid: typing.List[typing.List[int]], k: int) -> typing.List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the lexicographically minimal sequence of values of a path of length `k`\n    on the given `grid`. The path can start from any cell, move only to\n    orthogonally adjacent cells, and may revisit cells.\n    All values in `grid` are distinct and form the set {1, 2, ..., N*N}.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    grid : List[List[int]]\n        N x N grid with unique integers from 1 to N*N.\n    k : int\n        Desired length of the path (number of cells visited).\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    List[int]\n        The values on the cells along the minimal path.\n    \"\"\"\n    if k <= 0:\n        return []\n\n    n = len(grid)\n    if n == 0 or k > 10000:   # guard against extreme cases\n        return []\n\n    # Locate the cell containing the value 1 \u2013 the inevitable start.\n    start_i = start_j = None\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n):\n            if grid[i][j] == 1:\n                start_i, start_j = i, j\n                break\n        if start_i is not None:\n            break\n\n    # If 1 is missing (should not happen given the constraints) return empty.\n    if start_i is None:\n        return []\n\n    # Precompute neighbor offsets.\n    neighbors = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]\n\n    path_vals = [1]\n    cur_i, cur_j = start_i, start_j\n\n    for _ in range(1, k):          # already placed first cell\n        min_val = None\n        next_i = next_j = None\n        for di, dj in neighbors:\n            ni, nj = cur_i + di, cur_j + dj\n            if 0 <= ni < n and 0 <= nj < n:\n                val = grid[ni][nj]\n                if min_val is None or val < min_val:\n                    min_val = val\n                    next_i, next_j = ni, nj\n        # Append the chosen value and move current position.\n        path_vals.append(min_val)\n        cur_i, cur_j = next_i, next_j\n\n    return path_vals\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/130", "solution": "import sys\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Optional\n\n# Increase recursion limit for deep recursive calls\nsys.setrecursionlimit(200_000)\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Repository for memoization\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------\nclass MemoizationRepository:\n    \"\"\"\n    Simple in\u2011memory memoization repository for integer results.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._cache: Dict[int, int] = {}\n\n    def get(self, index: int) -> Optional[int]:\n        return self._cache.get(index)\n\n    def store(self, index: int, value: int) -> None:\n        self._cache[index] = value\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Interface and abstract base\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------\nclass ITribonacciCalculator:\n    \"\"\"\n    Interface for any Tribonacci sequence calculator.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def compute_tribonacci(self, n: int) -> List[int]:\n        raise NotImplementedError\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Concrete implementation\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------\nclass TribonacciCalculator(ITribonacciCalculator):\n    \"\"\"\n    Memoized recursive calculator for the custom Tribonacci sequence.\n\n    Core recurrence:\n        tri(0) = 1\n        tri(1) = 3\n        tri(n) = 1 + n // 2                if n is even\n        tri(n) = tri(n-1) + tri(n-2) + tri(n+1)   if n is odd\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, repo: MemoizationRepository | None = None) -> None:\n        self._repo = repo or MemoizationRepository()\n\n    def compute_tribonacci(self, n: int) -> List[int]:\n        self._validate_input(n)\n\n        # Ensure base values exist in cache\n        self._repo.store(0, 1)\n        self._repo.store(1, 3)\n\n        result: List[int] = []\n        for i in range(n + 1):\n            result.append(self._trib(i))\n        return result\n\n    def _validate_input(self, n: int) -> None:\n        if n < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"n must be non\u2011negative\")\n\n    def _trib(self, k: int) -> int:\n        cached = self._repo.get(k)\n        if cached is not None:\n            return cached\n\n        if k == 0:\n            value = 1\n        elif k == 1:\n            value = 3\n        elif k % 2 == 0:\n            value = 1 + k // 2\n        else:\n            # tri(k) for odd k: tri(k-1) + tri(k-2) + tri(k+1)\n            value = (\n                self._trib(k - 1) + self._trib(k - 2) + self._trib(k + 1)\n            )\n\n        self._repo.store(k, value)\n        return value\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Public helper function\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef generate_tribonacci_sequence(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return a list containing the first n + 1 Tribonacci numbers.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    n : int\n        Zero\u2011based upper bound of the sequence.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    List[int]\n        Sequence [tri(0), tri(1), \u2026, tri(n)].\n\n    Raises\n    ------\n    ValueError\n        If ``n`` is negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    return TribonacciCalculator().compute_tribonacci(n)\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/131", "solution": "def digits(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return the product of the odd digits of a positive integer `n`.\n    Return 0 if `n` contains no odd digits.\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> digits(1)\n    1\n    >>> digits(4)\n    0\n    >>> digits(235)\n    15\n    \"\"\"\n    product = 1\n    has_odd = False\n\n    while n:\n        digit = n % 10\n        if digit % 2:          # odd digit\n            product *= digit\n            has_odd = True\n        n //= 10\n\n    return product if has_odd else 0\n\n# Quick test\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    import doctest\n    doctest.testmod()"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "def is_nested(string):\n    \"\"\"Return True if there exists a valid subsequence of brackets in *string* that contains at least one nested pair.\n\n    The string is guaranteed to contain only '[' and ']'.\n    A valid subsequence must be a balanced bracket sequence. For nested we require at least two opening brackets before two closing brackets\n    in the same order that allows the outer pair to encompass the inner pair.\n\n    Algorithm\n    ---------\n    * Iterate over the string from left to right, keeping count of how many opening\n      brackets have been seen so far (`left_open`).\n    * Pre\u2011compute an array `right_suffix` where `right_suffix[i]` is the number of\n      closing brackets in `string[i:]`.\n    * For each position `i` where `string[i]` is an opening bracket:\n        * If we have seen at least one opening bracket before `i` (`left_open > 0`)\n          then there exists an earlier opening bracket that could be the start of a\n          nested subsequence.\n        * The remaining requirement is that there are at least two closing\n          brackets after `i`; i.e. `right_suffix[i+1] >= 2`.\n        * If both conditions hold, we can select a valid nested subsequence and\n          return True.\n    * If no such position exists, return False.\n\n    Complexity\n    -----------\n    Time: `O(n)` \u2013 a single pass to build the suffix counts and a second pass to\n           examine positions.\n    Space: `O(n)` \u2013 for the suffix array.  (Can be reduced to O(1) but clarity\n            is preferred.)\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    string : str\n        A string containing only '[' and ']'.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    bool\n        True if a nested subsequence exists, otherwise False.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(string)\n    if n < 4:\n        # Must have at least 4 brackets to form a nested subsequence\n        return False\n\n    # right_suffix[i] will contain the number of ']' in string[i:]\n    # Initialize with n+1 elements, where right_suffix[n] = 0\n    right_suffix = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for idx in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        right_suffix[idx] = right_suffix[idx + 1] + (1 if string[idx] == ']' else 0)\n\n    left_open = 0\n    for i, ch in enumerate(string):\n        if ch == '[':\n            # Condition 1: We have seen at least one opening bracket before 'i'.\n            # Condition 2: There are at least two closing brackets after 'i'.\n            if left_open > 0 and right_suffix[i + 1] >= 2:\n                return True\n            left_open += 1\n        elif ch == ']':\n            # Closing brackets do not affect left_open counter as per algorithm.\n            pass\n        else:\n            # The problem statement guarantees only '[' and ']', but raise for safety.\n            raise ValueError(\"String contains invalid characters\")\n\n    return False"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/133", "solution": "import math\nfrom typing import Iterable, List, Union\n\ndef sum_squares(lst: Iterable[Union[int, float]]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return the sum of the squares of the ceiling of each number in ``lst``.\n\n    The function rounds each element to the next integer greater than or\n    equal to the value (i.e. the mathematical ceiling).  The result of each\n    squaring operation is summed and the final result is returned as an\n    integer.\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> sum_squares([1, 2, 3])\n    14\n    >>> sum_squares([1, 4, 9])\n    98\n    >>> sum_squares([1, 3, 5, 7])\n    84\n    >>> sum_squares([1.4, 4.2, 0])\n    29\n    >>> sum_squares([-2.4, 1, 1])\n    6\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    lst : Iterable[Union[int, float]]\n        Iterable containing numeric values which may be integers or floats.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    int\n        Sum of squared ceilings.\n\n    Raises\n    ------\n    TypeError\n        If any element in ``lst`` cannot be converted to a float.\n    \"\"\"\n    total = 0\n    for value in lst:\n        try:\n            number = float(value)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError) as exc:\n            raise TypeError(f\"All list elements must be real numbers, got: {value!r}\") from exc\n\n        ceiled = math.ceil(number)\n        total += ceiled ** 2\n\n    return int(total)  # ensure return type is int even if total is float from arithmetic operations\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/134", "solution": "```python\n# alphabetic_checker/checker.py\nimport logging\nimport unicodedata\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Protocol, Optional, Any\n\n\n# --- Error Reporting ---\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass ExceptionDetail:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates detailed information about an exception.\"\"\"\n    message: str\n    code: Optional[str] = None\n\n\nclass InvalidInputException(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Exception raised for invalid input provided to the AlphabeticChecker.\n    Wraps an ExceptionDetail object for richer error information.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, detail: ExceptionDetail):\n        super().__init__(detail.message)\n        self.detail = detail\n\n\n# --- Validation Layer ---\nclass InputValidator:\n    \"\"\"Provides static methods for validating input values.\n    Ensures that inputs conform to expected types and non-null constraints.\n    \"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def validate(value: Any) -> Any:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates that the given value is a non-null string.\n\n        Args:\n            value: The value to validate.\n\n        Returns:\n            The original value if it passes validation.\n\n        Raises:\n            InvalidInputException: If the value is None or not a string.\n        \"\"\"\n        if value is None:\n            raise InvalidInputException(\n                detail=ExceptionDetail(\n                    message=\"Input cannot be None.\",\n                    code=\"INPUT_IS_NONE\"\n                )\n            )\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            raise InvalidInputException(\n                detail=ExceptionDetail(\n                    message=f\"Input must be a string, but got {type(value).__name__}.\",\n                    code=\"INPUT_NOT_STRING\"\n                )\n            )\n        return value\n\n\n# --- Classification Layer ---\nclass IUnicodeClassifier(Protocol):\n    \"\"\"Protocol defining the interface for Unicode character classification.\n    Implementations determine if a given character has the Unicode Letter property.\n    \"\"\"\n    def is_letter(self, char: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Determines if the given character is a Unicode letter.\n\n        Args:\n            char: A single Unicode character string.\n\n        Returns:\n            True if the character is a letter, False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        ...\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass PythonUnicodeClassifier(IUnicodeClassifier):\n    \"\"\"Concrete implementation of IUnicodeClassifier using Python's `unicodedata` module.\n    A character is considered a letter if its Unicode general category starts with 'L'.\n    \"\"\"\n    def is_letter(self, char: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Determines if the given character is a Unicode letter based on its\n        general category ('L' for Letter).\n\n        Args:\n            char: A single Unicode character string.\n\n        Returns:\n            True if the character's category starts with 'L', False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        category = unicodedata.category(char)\n        return category.startswith('L')\n\n\n# --- Service Layer ---\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass AlphabeticChecker:\n    \"\"\"A language-agnostic library for validating if a string's final character\n    has the Unicode Letter property. Provides deterministic, stateless,\n    and thread-safe behavior.\n    \"\"\"\n    _unicode_classifier: IUnicodeClassifier = field(default_factory=PythonUnicodeClassifier)\n    _logger: logging.Logger = field(default_factory=lambda: logging.getLogger(__name__))\n\n    def is_last_char_letter(self, input_string: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Determines if the final character of the input string has the Unicode Letter property.\n\n        Args:\n            input_string: The string to examine.\n\n        Returns:\n            True if the final character is a Unicode letter, False otherwise.\n\n        Raises:\n            InvalidInputException: If `input_string` is None or not a string.\n        \"\"\"\n        # 1. Validate input\n        valid_string = InputValidator.validate(input_string)\n\n        # 2. Handle empty string corner case\n        if not valid_string:  # equivalent to valid_string == \"\"\n            self._logger.debug(\"Input string is empty. Returning False.\")\n            return False\n\n        # 3. Get the last character\n        last_char = valid_string[-1]\n\n        # 4. Delegate classification\n        is_letter = self._unicode_classifier.is_letter(last_char)\n\n        # 5. Log and return\n        self._logger.debug(\n            f\"Checked last char '{last_char}' (U+{ord(last_char):04X}): is_letter={is_letter}\"\n        )\n        return is_letter\n\n\n# alphabetic_checker/__init__.py\n# (This file can be empty or contain package-level initialization like version info)\n\nfrom .checker import (\n    AlphabeticChecker,\n    PythonUnicodeClassifier,\n    IUnicodeClassifier,\n    InvalidInputException,\n    ExceptionDetail,\n    InputValidator\n)\n\n\n# alphabetic_checker/cli.py\nimport sys\nimport logging\nfrom .checker import AlphabeticChecker, PythonUnicodeClassifier, InvalidInputException\n\n\ndef main():\n    \"\"\"Command-line interface for AlphabeticChecker.\n    Reads a string from command-line arguments and prints whether its last character is a letter.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Configure logging for the CLI, defaulting to INFO level\n    logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=\"%(levelname)s: %(message)s\")\n    logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\n    if len(sys.argv) < 2:\n        logger.error(\"Usage: alphabetic_checker <string_to_check>\")\n        sys.exit(1)\n\n    input_string = sys.argv[1]\n\n    # Initialize AlphabeticChecker with default classifier and specific logger\n    checker = AlphabeticChecker(\n        _unicode_classifier=PythonUnicodeClassifier(),\n        _logger=logger,\n    )\n\n    try:\n        result = checker.is_last_char_letter(input_string)\n        print(result)\n    except InvalidInputException as e:\n        logger.error(f\"Error: {e.detail.message} (Code: {e.detail.code})\")\n        sys.exit(1)\n    except Exception as e:\n        logger.error(f\"An unexpected error occurred: {e}\")\n        sys.exit(1)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/135", "solution": "def can_arrange(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Return the largest index of an element that is strictly less than the element immediately preceding it.\n\n    The function scans the list from left to right, keeping track of the last index\n    where arr[i] < arr[i-1]. If no such index exists, -1 is returned.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    arr : list[int]\n        The input list. It is assumed to contain no duplicate values.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    int\n        The largest qualifying index, or -1 if none.\n    \"\"\"\n    last_index = -1\n    for i in range(1, len(arr)):\n        if arr[i] < arr[i - 1]:\n            last_index = i\n    return last_index"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/136", "solution": "from typing import Iterable, Tuple, Optional\n\n__all__ = [\"largest_smallest_integers\"]\n\n\ndef largest_smallest_integers(lst: Iterable[int]) -> Tuple[Optional[int], Optional[int]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return the largest negative integer and the smallest positive integer from a list.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    lst : Iterable[int]\n        Iterable of integers to inspect.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    Tuple[Optional[int], Optional[int]]\n        The first element is the largest negative integer (None if there is none).\n        The second element is the smallest positive integer (None if there is none).\n\n    Notes\n    -----\n    * Zero is ignored for both calculations.\n    * If ``lst`` is empty or contains only zero, both values will be ``None``.\n    \"\"\"\n    largest_negative: Optional[int] = None\n    smallest_positive: Optional[int] = None\n\n    for n in lst:\n        if n < 0:\n            if largest_negative is None or n > largest_negative:\n                largest_negative = n\n        elif n > 0:\n            if smallest_positive is None or n < smallest_positive:\n                smallest_positive = n\n\n    return largest_negative, smallest_positive\n\n# Example usage:\n# print(largest_smallest_integers([2, 4, 1, 3, 5, 7]))  # (None, 1)\n# print(largest_smallest_integers([-10, -5, 3, 2]))       # (-5, 2)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/137", "solution": "def compare_one(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Return the larger of *a* or *b*, preserving the original type.\n\n    Arguments can be integers, floats, or strings that represent real\n    numbers.  Strings may contain a decimal separator of either `.` or `,`,\n    e.g. `\"2,3\"`.\n\n    If the numeric values are equal, the function returns `None`.\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> compare_one(1, 2.5)\n    2.5\n    >>> compare_one(1, \"2,3\")\n    '2,3'\n    >>> compare_one(\"5,1\", \"6\")\n    '6'\n    >>> compare_one(\"1\", 1)\n    None\n    \"\"\"\n    def to_number(value):\n        \"\"\"Convert *value* to a float for numeric comparison.\n\n        - Integers and floats are returned as `float(value)`.\n        - Strings are assumed to represent a number.  They may use a comma\n          as the decimal separator.\n        - All other types raise a `ValueError`.\n        \"\"\"\n        if isinstance(value, (int, float)):\n            return float(value)\n        if isinstance(value, str):\n            # replace comma with dot to support both decimal separators\n            try:\n                return float(value.replace(',', '.'))\n            except ValueError:\n                raise ValueError(f\"String '{value}' is not a valid number\")\n        raise ValueError(f\"Unsupported type {type(value).__name__} for comparison\")\n\n    num_a = to_number(a)\n    num_b = to_number(b)\n\n    # Determine numeric relationship\n    if num_a == num_b:\n        return None\n    return a if num_a > num_b else b"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/138", "solution": "def is_equal_to_sum_even(n):\n    \"\"\"Determine if an integer n can be expressed as the sum of exactly four\n    positive even integers.\n\n    Args:\n        n: An integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if n can be expressed as the sum of exactly four positive even\n        integers, False otherwise.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If n is not an integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an integer\")\n\n    # Minimum sum of four positive even integers is 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8.\n    if n < 8:\n        return False\n\n    # Sum of even numbers is always even; odd n cannot be represented.\n    if n % 2 != 0:\n        return False\n\n    # For n >= 8 and even, it's always possible:\n    # Let a = n/2 - 4, b = c = d = 0.\n    # Then 2(a+1) + 2(b+1) + 2(c+1) + 2(d+1) = 2a + 8 = n.\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/139", "solution": "#!/usr/bin/env python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n\n# ==============================\n# src/fpc/domain/positive_integer.py\n# ==============================\nclass PositiveInteger:\n    \"\"\"Validate that an input is a natural number (>0).\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, value: int):\n        self.value = value\n\n    def is_positive(self) -> bool:\n        return isinstance(self.value, int) and self.value > 0\n\n\n# ==============================\n# src/fpc/domain/factorial.py\n# ==============================\nclass Factorial:\n    \"\"\"Compute k! for any integer k \u2265 1.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, k: int):\n        self.k = k\n\n    def compute(self) -> int:\n        if self.k == 1:\n            return 1\n        return self.k * Factorial(self.k - 1).compute()\n\n\n# ==============================\n# src/fpc/domain/product_of_factorials.py\n# ==============================\nclass ProductOfFactorials:\n    \"\"\"Compute \u220f_{i=1}^{n} i!.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, n: int):\n        self.n = n\n\n    def compute(self) -> int:\n        # Pure domain logic (no caching); used by design, though not by the service in this layout.\n        result = 1\n        for i in range(1, self.n + 1):\n            result *= Factorial(i).compute()\n        return result\n\n\n# ==============================\n# src/fpc/infrastructure/cache.py\n# ==============================\nimport threading\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int = 128):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache = OrderedDict()\n        self.lock = threading.RLock()  # Reentrant lock for thread safety\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        with self.lock:\n            if key not in self.cache:\n                return None\n            self.cache.move_to_end(key)  # Mark as recently used\n            return self.cache[key]\n\n    def set(self, key, value):\n        with self.lock:\n            self.cache[key] = value\n            self.cache.move_to_end(key)  # Mark as recently used\n            if len(self.cache) > self.capacity:\n                self.cache.popitem(last=False)  # Remove least recently used\n\n\nclass FactorialCache(LRUCache):\n    pass\n\n\nclass POFCache(LRUCache):\n    pass\n\n\n# ==============================\n# src/fpc/application/factorial_service.py\n# ==============================\nfrom fpc.domain.factorial import Factorial\nfrom fpc.infrastructure.cache import FactorialCache\n\n\nclass FactorialService:\n    def __init__(self, cache: FactorialCache):\n        self.cache = cache\n\n    def get_factorial(self, k: int) -> int:\n        val = self.cache.get(k)\n        if val is None:\n            val = Factorial(k).compute()\n            self.cache.set(k, val)\n        return val\n\n\n# ==============================\n# src/fpc/application/pof_service.py\n# ==============================\nfrom fpc.infrastructure.cache import POFCache\nfrom fpc.application.factorial_service import FactorialService\n\n\nclass POFService:\n    def __init__(self, cache: POFCache, factorial_service: FactorialService):\n        self.cache = cache\n        self.factorial_service = factorial_service\n\n    def get_pof(self, n: int) -> int:\n        val = self.cache.get(n)\n        if val is None:\n            prod = 1\n            for i in range(1, n + 1):\n                prod *= self.factorial_service.get_factorial(i)\n            self.cache.set(n, prod)\n            val = prod\n        return val\n\n\n# ==============================\n# src/fpc/api/controller.py\n# ==============================\nfrom fpc.domain.positive_integer import PositiveInteger\nfrom fpc.application.pof_service import POFService\n\n\nclass FPCController:\n    def __init__(self, pof_service: POFService):\n        self.pof_service = pof_service\n\n    def product_of_factorials(self, n: int) -> int:\n        if not PositiveInteger(n).is_positive():\n            raise ValueError(\"n must be a positive integer\")\n        return self.pof_service.get_pof(n)\n\n\n# ==============================\n# src/fpc/bootstrap.py\n# ==============================\nfrom fpc.infrastructure.cache import FactorialCache, POFCache\nfrom fpc.application.factorial_service import FactorialService\nfrom fpc.application.pof_service import POFService\nfrom fpc.api.controller import FPCController\n\n\ndef _build_controller() -> FPCController:\n    \"\"\"Composition root \u2013 wires dependencies.\"\"\"\n    f_cache = FactorialCache()\n    pof_cache = POFCache()\n    f_service = FactorialService(f_cache)\n    pof_service = POFService(pof_cache, f_service)\n    return FPCController(pof_service)\n\n\nproduct_of_factorials = _build_controller().product_of_factorials\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/140", "solution": "import re\n\ndef fix_spaces(text: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Replace spaces with underscores; groups of more than two consecutive spaces are replaced with a single hyphen.\"\"\"\n    def repl(match: re.Match) -> str:\n        space_seq = match.group()\n        return '-' if len(space_seq) > 2 else '_' * len(space_seq)\n\n    return re.sub(r' +', repl, text)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/141", "solution": "def file_name_check(file_name: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validate a file name according to the following rules:\n    1. The name must contain exactly one dot '.' separating the base name and the extension.\n    2. The base name must not be empty and must start with a Latin letter (a-z or A-Z).\n    3. The extension must be one of ['txt', 'exe', 'dll'].\n    4. The total number of digits in the entire file name must not exceed three.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    file_name : str\n        The file name to validate.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    str\n        'Yes' if the file name is valid, 'No' otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Exactly one dot\n    if file_name.count('.') != 1:\n        return 'No'\n\n    base, ext = file_name.split('.', 1)\n\n    # 2. Base name non-empty and starts with a letter\n    if not base or not base[0].isalpha():\n        return 'No'\n\n    # 3. Valid extension\n    if ext not in {'txt', 'exe', 'dll'}:\n        return 'No'\n\n    # 4. At most three digits in the whole file name\n    digits_count = sum(ch.isdigit() for ch in file_name)\n    if digits_count > 3:\n        return 'No'\n\n    return 'Yes'"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/142", "solution": "def sum_squares(start: int, end: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of squares of all integers within a specified inclusive range.\n\n    Args:\n        start: The beginning of the inclusive range.\n        end: The end of the inclusive range.\n\n    Returns:\n        The sum of squares of integers from 'start' to 'end' (inclusive).\n        Returns 0 if 'start' is greater than 'end' (representing an empty range).\n    \"\"\"\n    if start > end:\n        return 0\n\n    return sum(i * i for i in range(start, end + 1))"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/143", "solution": "def words_in_sentence(sentence: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return the words from the original sentence whose lengths are prime numbers,\n    preserving the original order.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    sentence : str\n        A sentence containing only letters and spaces.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    str\n        A space\u2010separated string of words with prime length. If no such words\n        exist, an empty string is returned.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Check if an integer n > 0 is a prime number.\"\"\"\n        if n < 2:\n            return False\n        if n == 2:\n            return True\n        if n % 2 == 0:\n            return False\n        divisor = 3\n        while divisor * divisor <= n:\n            if n % divisor == 0:\n                return False\n            divisor += 2\n        return True\n\n    return \" \".join(word for word in sentence.split() if _is_prime(len(word)))"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/144", "solution": "def simplify(x: str, n: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns True if the product of the fractions represented by the strings x and n\n    is a whole number, False otherwise.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    x : str\n        A string in the form \"<numerator>/<denominator>\" representing a fraction.\n    n : str\n        A string in the form \"<numerator>/<denominator>\" representing a fraction.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    bool\n        True if the product is an integer, False otherwise.\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> simplify(\"1/5\", \"5/1\")\n    True\n    >>> simplify(\"1/6\", \"2/1\")\n    False\n    >>> simplify(\"7/10\", \"10/2\")\n    False\n    \"\"\"\n    def parse_fraction(frac: str):\n        numerator, denominator = frac.split(\"/\")\n        return int(numerator), int(denominator)\n\n    a, b = parse_fraction(x)\n    c, d = parse_fraction(n)\n\n    product_numerator = a * c\n    product_denominator = b * d\n\n    return product_numerator % product_denominator == 0\n\n# Basic test cases\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    assert simplify(\"1/5\", \"5/1\") is True\n    assert simplify(\"1/6\", \"2/1\") is False\n    assert simplify(\"7/10\", \"10/2\") is False\n    assert simplify(\"3/4\", \"8/9\") is False\n    assert simplify(\"2/3\", \"3/2\") is True\n    print(\"All tests passed.\")"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/145", "solution": "def order_by_points(nums: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sort the given list of integers in ascending order based on the sum of their digits.\n    If multiple integers have the same digit sum, their relative order in the original list is preserved.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    nums : list[int]\n        The list of integers to sort.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    list[int]\n        The sorted list according to the criteria.\n\n    Examples\n    --------\n    >>> order_by_points([1, 11, -1, -11, -12])\n    [1, -1, 11, -11, -12]\n    >>> order_by_points([])\n    []\n    \"\"\"\n    def digit_sum(n: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Return the sum of the absolute digits of n.\"\"\"\n        return sum(int(d) for d in str(abs(n)))\n\n    # Enumerate to keep original indices for stable sort\n    indexed = list(enumerate(nums))\n    # Sort by digit_sum and original index\n    indexed.sort(key=lambda x: (digit_sum(x[1]), x[0]))\n    # Extract sorted numbers\n    return [num for _, num in indexed]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/146", "solution": "def specialFilter(nums):\n    \"\"\"Return the count of numbers in ``nums`` that are greater than 10 and\n    have odd digits at both the first and last positions.\n\n    The function treats negative numbers the same as positive ones with\n    respect to digit extraction \u2013 the sign is ignored when determining the\n    first and last digits.\n\n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    nums : Iterable[int]\n        An iterable of integers.\n\n    Returns\n    -------\n    int\n        The number of integers satisfying the conditions.\n    \"\"\"\n    def is_odd_digit(d: int) -> bool:\n        return d in {1, 3, 5, 7, 9}\n\n    count = 0\n    for n in nums:\n        if n <= 10:\n            continue\n        abs_n = abs(n)\n        s = str(abs_n)\n        first_digit = int(s[0])\n        last_digit = int(s[-1])\n        if is_odd_digit(first_digit) and is_odd_digit(last_digit):\n            count += 1\n    return count\n\n# Example usage:\n# print(specialFilter([15, -73, 14, -15]))  # Output: 1\n# print(specialFilter([33, -2, -3, 45, 21, 109]))  # Output: 2\n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/147", "solution": "def get_max_triples(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compute the number of triples (a[i], a[j], a[k]) such that\n    i < j < k and a[i] + a[j] + a[k] is divisible by 3.\n    \n    The array a is defined by a[i] = i*i - i + 1 for 1-indexed i.\n    \n    Observation: a[i] modulo 3 depends only on i modulo 3.\n        i % 3 == 0 or 1  -> a[i] % 3 == 1\n        i % 3 == 2       -> a[i] % 3 == 0\n    \n    Hence the only residues available are 0 and 1.\n    A sum of three such residues is divisible by 3 if they are all 0 or all 1.\n    Therefore the answer is the number of 3\u2011combinations of indices yielding\n    residue 0 plus the number of 3\u2011combinations yielding residue 1.\n    \n    Parameters\n    ----------\n    n : int\n        Length of the array.\n    \n    Returns\n    -------\n    int\n        Count of valid triples.\n    \"\"\"\n    def comb3(x: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Return x choose 3 (0 if x < 3).\"\"\"\n        return x * (x - 1) * (x - 2) // 6 if x >= 3 else 0\n\n    # Count indices with residue 0 (i % 3 == 2).\n    count0 = (n + 1) // 3          # positions 2,5,8,...\n    # Remaining indices have residue 1.\n    count1 = n - count0\n\n    return comb3(count0) + comb3(count1)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/148", "solution": "def bf(planet1, planet2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Return a tuple of planets that lie between two given planets in the solar system\n    (ordered from the Sun outward). If either planet name is invalid, return an empty tuple.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Ordered list of planets from the Sun\n    planets = [\n        \"Mercury\",\n        \"Venus\",\n        \"Earth\",\n        \"Mars\",\n        \"Jupiter\",\n        \"Saturn\",\n        \"Uranus\",\n        \"Neptune\",\n    ]\n\n    # Validate input planets\n    if planet1 not in planets or planet2 not in planets:\n        return tuple()\n\n    # Determine indices\n    idx1 = planets.index(planet1)\n    idx2 = planets.index(planet2)\n\n    # If planet1 comes before planet2\n    if idx1 < idx2:\n        between = planets[idx1 + 1:idx2]\n    else:\n        # planet1 comes after planet2\n        between = planets[idx2 + 1:idx1]\n\n    return tuple(between)"}
